If you do not have a physical hardware token, using the TPM to enroll a secret
is highly recommended. To the user, the password prompt will be very similar to
the existing prompt but TPM provides much stronger protection behind the scenes.
It can be used to only make the encryption keys available if Secure Boot has not
been tampered with and the booted UKI is in an expected state. This setup is
similar to how Windows protects the system by default. We will employ a slightly
different approach, but more on that later.

The TPM can also be used to secure a password, providing stronger protection
than a regular software-based approach, although the particulars of this depend
on the firmware implementation. In general, TPM-enclosed passwords can have much
lower entropy as the TPM will lock after too many failed attempts and thus
prevent brute-force attempts.

This script will only enroll TPM-based secrets along with a PIN. A TPM that
unlocks a root partition without any further protective measures offers only
the most basic protection in the event of a stolen system. However, combined
with a low to medium entropy PIN, enrolling the TPM as a key offers strong
protection against boot chain tampering as well as unauthorized physical
access.
